// `[using import(I) definition(N) I.sym]` — citing a definition CLAUSE across an import.
//
// REGRESSION. This did not resolve: the parser accepted the syntax (`definition(N)` gates on
// `isTheoryRule`, which includes `import`) but neither the read pass nor the process phase
// MANGLED the name, so the demand resolved to the predicate rather than to its clause axiom
// and reported "names an identifier, not an axiom/theorem".
//
// Two sites had to mangle, mirroring the same-file `[by definition(N) f]` path:
//   - RefScan.zig's `.import` arm, which emitted c.refs[0] verbatim (its `.definition`
//     sibling had always mangled);
//   - Prove.zig's import-citation handler, before `resolveFactIn`.
//
// And the mangle must use the token's NAME, not its source span: a qualified token keeps the
// qualifier in its own field, so the span is `lib.p` while the name is the bare `p` the
// clause was published from. The span would build `definition{lib.p}{0}`, matching nothing.
//
// Found while formalizing a definition declared in a shared vocabulary file and consumed in
// another — the multi-file shape that motivates having a definition in one place at all.
theory lib <<< "cross_file_definition_lib.b4m"

const a = lib.a
pred p = lib.p

intheory usesIt

theorem usesIt: forall x: lib.T; p(x) iff x = a
proof
  @conclusion |
    forall x: lib.T; p(x) iff x = a
    [using import(lib) definition(0) lib.p]
qed
